# /opt/vpntunnel/.env — runtime flags for the vpntunnel systemd unit.
#
# Copy this file to /opt/vpntunnel/.env on the host (the runtime file is named
# .env, matching the release-layout convention). systemd EnvironmentFile syntax:
# KEY=value, one per line, no quotes, no spaces around '='. It is OPERATOR-MANAGED
# and edited by hand — the release workflow does NOT rewrite it (the CI deploy user
# cannot write the base dir), and the values are stable across deploys because the
# unit's ExecStart points at the fixed bin/release channel symlink. It holds no
# secrets (only paths and a hostname). systemd (root) reads it before launching the
# service and passes the values as the process environment, so the service user
# never reads this file: keep it root:root 0600. EnvironmentFile is re-read on every
# restart, so a change here needs only `systemctl restart vpntunnel`, no daemon-reload.
#
# VPNTUNNEL_TLS_CERT_DIR must be non-empty in production — an empty value runs
# the API as plain HTTP (the post-deploy HTTPS health-check would then fail).
VPNTUNNEL_CONFIG_PATH=/opt/vpntunnel/configs/proxy.json
VPNTUNNEL_TLS_CERT_DIR=/opt/vpntunnel/configs/tls/
VPNTUNNEL_TLS_CERT_HOST=localhost

# VPNTUNNEL_TELEGRAMBOT_DSN — optional Telegram notification on tunnel change
# (startup, reconnect, on-demand zone switch). Unset = notifications disabled;
# a malformed value only warns and disables, it never blocks startup. Format:
#   tbot://<adminChatID>:@<botToken>/
# where <botToken> is the full "<bot-id>:<secret>" string BotFather gives you.
# This value is a SECRET (it grants send access to the target chat) — it holds
# no rotation-visible marker in logs (only token_len and enabled/disabled state
# are ever logged), same handling as VPNTUNNEL_TLS_CERT_DIR above: the operator
# adds it here by hand on the host (the CI deploy user cannot write this file)
# and runs `systemctl restart vpntunnel` to apply it.
#VPNTUNNEL_TELEGRAMBOT_DSN=tbot://<adminChatID>:@<botToken>/
